WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.840 --> 00:00:09.720
The Educational Justice
Institute, or TEJI,

00:00:09.720 --> 00:00:13.050
provides college-level
courses to incarcerated people

00:00:13.050 --> 00:00:15.720
using a mixed classroom model.

00:00:15.720 --> 00:00:18.630
Meaning, half of the
students in a given course

00:00:18.630 --> 00:00:21.450
will be incarcerated
students, and the other half

00:00:21.450 --> 00:00:23.550
will be MIT students.

00:00:23.550 --> 00:00:25.560
I mean, by the time you
reach the classroom,

00:00:25.560 --> 00:00:29.130
there are probably three
or four locked doors

00:00:29.130 --> 00:00:32.189
that you have to go
through to get out.

00:00:32.189 --> 00:00:35.190
The mixture of these two
populations in those classes

00:00:35.190 --> 00:00:39.280
is really wonderfully
moving and enlightening.

00:00:39.280 --> 00:00:41.040
We all learn a lot
from each other.

00:00:41.040 --> 00:00:43.200
People from very, very
different backgrounds--

00:00:43.200 --> 00:00:46.470
very, very different
life experiences.

00:00:46.470 --> 00:00:50.100
These courses will focus
on things like forgiveness,

00:00:50.100 --> 00:00:52.020
or anger, or love--

00:00:52.020 --> 00:00:55.420
things that we all share.

00:00:55.420 --> 00:00:57.560
Marissa has been with
us from the beginning,

00:00:57.560 --> 00:01:00.940
from her freshman year, and
she's now a graduate student.

00:01:00.940 --> 00:01:05.980
She's in many ways one of
the founders of the program,

00:01:05.980 --> 00:01:09.940
because she's always been
completely reliable, and so

00:01:09.940 --> 00:01:12.290
hardworking.

00:01:12.290 --> 00:01:15.640
I got to hear how
impactful education can

00:01:15.640 --> 00:01:17.710
be for incarcerated people.

00:01:17.710 --> 00:01:21.490
I still remember on the
first class I ever went to

00:01:21.490 --> 00:01:27.310
in a prison, one man told me how
prior to his experience taking

00:01:27.310 --> 00:01:30.040
courses in prison,
he had never felt

00:01:30.040 --> 00:01:34.000
like anyone had believed in
him before in his entire life.

00:01:34.000 --> 00:01:37.420
And this man was
probably in his 40s.

00:01:37.420 --> 00:01:39.460
Forming that kind of
connection with people

00:01:39.460 --> 00:01:43.240
and seeing yourself really make
a difference in their lives

00:01:43.240 --> 00:01:46.430
is just tremendously rewarding.

00:01:46.430 --> 00:01:48.430
I think in a few years,
we're going to look back

00:01:48.430 --> 00:01:52.060
and we're going to see that
MIT made an enormous difference

00:01:52.060 --> 00:01:55.450
in prison education, and
in the culture of prisons

00:01:55.450 --> 00:01:56.218
in the state.

00:01:56.218 --> 00:01:57.760
And really now
throughout New England

00:01:57.760 --> 00:02:01.870
because we're working in Maine
and New Hampshire as well.

00:02:01.870 --> 00:02:04.090
If you put together
a community of people

00:02:04.090 --> 00:02:07.870
who are passionate about
an issue you can really

00:02:07.870 --> 00:02:12.160
make a change, and
in the case of TEJI,

00:02:12.160 --> 00:02:16.150
we seem to have the right
skills and resources at MIT

00:02:16.150 --> 00:02:20.050
to really make an impact
in this specific way.

00:02:20.050 --> 00:02:23.170
Everyone has something
that they can offer,

00:02:23.170 --> 00:02:26.680
and it's just a matter
of putting together

00:02:26.680 --> 00:02:32.610
the right communities and
connections to make change.

